Port `src/shared/store/read/{nodes,edges,metrics}.ts` (161 + 245 + 53 lines). Signatures, with rows exactly as the TypeScript row types:
```rust
pub fn find_by_qn(conn: &Conn, qn: &str) -> Result<Option<NodeRow>>;
pub fn find_by_name(conn: &Conn, name: &str, kinds: Option<&[String]>) -> Result<Vec<NodeRow>>;
pub fn members(conn: &Conn, parent_id: i64) -> Result<Vec<NodeRow>>;
pub fn nodes_by_file(conn: &Conn, file_id: i64) -> Result<Vec<NodeRow>>;
pub fn inbound(conn: &Conn, node_id: i64, kinds: Option<&[String]>) -> Result<Vec<NeighbourEdge>>;
pub fn outbound(conn: &Conn, node_id: i64, kinds: Option<&[String]>) -> Result<Vec<NeighbourEdge>>;
pub fn metrics_for(conn: &Conn, node_id: i64) -> Result<Option<NodeMetrics>>;
```
`find_by_qn` is always called with an exact `qn` (the UNIQUE index `node_qn_unique`) and returns at most one row. `find_by_name` orders deterministically — copy the ordering of the TypeScript statement verbatim; a re-sorted result silently changes which callers a trace page shows. `members` returns source order (`start_line`, then `id`). `inbound`/`outbound` join the opposite node and the edge's `file_id` path so a caller can render `{file, line, kind, tier, qn}` without a second query; the `kinds` filter is optional and must not become a per-call dynamic string — build the statement once per arity through the prepared cache, keyed like `reader.inbound/3` as the TypeScript side does.
All statements go through `Conn::prepared(sql_key, …)` from TASK-008 — never `conn.prepare` inside a handler and never a query string built in a loop.
Tests build a tiny database with two files, a class with a method, an edge both ways and a metrics row, and assert: exact hit/miss for `find_by_qn`, ordering of `find_by_name` for a duplicate name, `members` order, kinds filtering, and that the second call of the same statement is a cache hit (`statement_cache_stats().hits == 1`).

